h2><p>I started using PostgreSQL roughly in 2003 for a research project cal=
led <a href=3D"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1087474/">Colum=
baDB</a>. Columba is no more, but PostgreSQL is still alive and kicking mor=
e than ever.</p><p>In 2003, MySQL was much more widely used than PostgreSQL=
. MySQL was also potentially faster as it did not implement all features of=
 the SQL standard. At the same time MySQL was lacking many features that we=
 needed (full-text search, powerful indexes, SQL standard compliance etc). =
PostgreSQL felt more like a =E2=80=9Creal=E2=80=9D database in comparison t=
o MySQL - like a tiny version of Oracle - but in open source clothes.</p><p=
>During that research project I learned a lot about databases, indexes and =
the power of PostgreSQL.
One important use-case was full-text search. We could have used MySQL in co=
njunction with another system like Lucene / Solr to make our database searc=
hable. But that would have meant running and maintaining two such systems. =
Complicated.</p><p>PostgreSQL allowed us to use a <a href=3D"https://www.po=
stgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch.html">fulltext search plugin</a> to do=
 everything in one system. No need to sync any data. No need to maintain an=
d run two systems. It just worked and made us smile (after some tweaks of c=
ourse). Simplicity.</p><p>Since then I used PostgreSQL for many use-cases t=
hroughout my career as <a href=3D"https://www.raphaelbauer.com/interim-cto/=
">CTO / Interim Manager</a>. Most recently I used PostgreSQL to store very =
high volume web analytics time series data via its <a href=3D"https://www.t=
imescale.com/">TimescaleDB plugin</a>. Check out <a href=3D"https://www.pri=
vatracker.com/">Privatracker - the best way to do web analytics and respect=
 the privacy of your visitors</a> - to see it in action.</p><p>Many others =
discussed the topic from different angles. And each article is really worth=
 your time (<a href=3D"https://lucumr.pocoo.org/2012/12/29/sql-is-agile/">S=
QL is Agile</a>, <a href=3D"https://www.amazingcto.com/postgres-for-everyth=
ing/">Stephan Schmidt on Using SQL for Everything</a>). Also check out my <=
a href=3D"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/raphaelabauer_postgresql-for-every=
thing-activity-7163447276114317313-mSrw?utm_source=3Dshare&amp;utm_medium=
=3Dmember_desktop">Linkedin post</a>.</p><p>And if you are using PostgreSQL=
 I can highly recommend reading Hazel Bachrach=E2=80=99s nice post on <a hr=
ef=3D"https://challahscript.com/what_i_wish_someone_told_me_about_postgres"=
>=E2=80=9CWhat I Wish Someone Told Me About Postgres=E2=80=9D</a>.</p><p>In=
 my humble opinion the power of PostgreSQL comes from three sources:</p><ol=
><li>It is <u>rock-solid and stable</u>.</li><li>It is <u>easy to run, inst=
all and scale</u>.</li><li>It massively <u>simplifies your IT setup</u> by =
being not only a RDBMS, but also a full-text search engine, a document stor=
age and much much more=E2=80=A6</li></ol><p>Let=E2=80=99s have a closer loo=
k=E2=80=A6</p><h2 id=3D"rock-solid-and-stable">Rock Solid and Stable</h2><p=
>PostgreSQL is boring old technology. <a href=3D"https://de.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/PostgreSQL">The first PostgreSQL release dates back to 1996</a>. Postgr=
eSQL is also very widely used - for a very long amount of time. Ironing out=
 bugs - especially in database systems - takes time. PostgreSQL had that ti=
me.</p><p>It also has a very active community that diligently adds more and=
 more features without breaking any old parts of it. In recent years Postgr=
eSQL got many amazing features like json document storage, partitioning sup=
port, common table expressions and much much more. Each new release of Post=
greSQL is exciting and brings new nice features.</p><p><u>True - PostgreSQL=
 is old - but the features are very very modern - and PostgreSQL becomes be=
tter with every release.</u></p><h2 id=3D"easy-to-run-install-and-scale">Ea=
sy to Run, Install and Scale</h2><p>PostgreSQL can be installed very easily=
 locally. It is bundled with all major Linux distributions, part of <a href=
=3D"https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/Homebrew">Mac brew</a>, but can also b=
e installed with applications like <a href=3D"https://postgresapp.com/">Pos=
tgresApp</a>.</p><p>When running tests, it comes in handy using <a href=3D"=
https://java.testcontainers.org/modules/databases/postgres/">Testcontainers=
 with PostgreSQL</a>. It was never easier running your tests against a real=
 PostgreSQL database that is 100% similar to the production thing.</p><p>If=
 you want to run PostgreSQL on a server then you can simply apt-get install=
 it. Or run it in a <a href=3D"https://hub.docker.com/_/postgres">docker co=
ntainer</a>.</p><p>All cloud providers allow you to run (and scale!) Postgr=
eSQL by clicking a single button. You got ample of choice at your fingertip=
s:</p><ul><li><a href=3D"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/postgresql/">Amazon AWS=
</a></li><li><a href=3D"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres">Google =
GCP</a></li><li><a href=3D"https://azure.microsoft.com/en-gb/products/postg=
resql/">Microsoft Azure</a></li><li><a href=3D"https://www.elephantsql.com/=
">ElephantSQL</a></li><li><a href=3D"https://www.crunchydata.com/">CrunchyD=
ata</a></li><li><a href=3D"https://www.timescale.com/">Timescale</a></li><l=
i>=E2=80=A6 and many more =E2=80=A6</li></ul><p><u>That makes PostgreSQL on=
e of the most widely supported software systems in the market. And for you =
this means less maintenance and more time for creating new features for cli=
ents.</u></p><h2 id=3D"simplifies-your-it-setup">Simplifies Your IT Setup</=
h2><p>Running PostgreSQL in the cloud is already just one click. But it get=
s even better. PostgreSQL can replace many systems that youd=E2=80=99d have=
 to run otherwise.</p><h3 id=3D"postgresql-replaces-solr-and-elastic-full-t=
ext-search">PostgreSQL Replaces Solr and Elastic: Full-Text Search</h3><p>P=
ostgreSQL allows you to turn your text data into user-searchable data. With=
out a separate system. It=E2=80=99s also language agnostic and you=E2=80=99=
ll never have any sync problems between your data and your fulltext search =
system.</p><p>The most impressive article on the topic is how <a href=3D"ht=
tps://www.contentful.com/blog/contentful-faster-full-text-search/">Contentf=
ul used PostgreSQL to enable fulltext search for their users</a>. It=E2=80=
=99s a tale in simplicity that enables growth.</p><p>Instacart did somethin=
g very similar: They <a href=3D"https://tech.instacart.com/how-instacart-bu=
ilt-a-modern-search-infrastructure-on-postgres-c528fa601d54">built their mo=
dern search infrastructure on Postgres</a> instead of running a separate se=
arch cluster. Same story, different company.</p><p>The built-in <a href=3D"=
https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-textsearch.html"><code>tsv=
ector</code> / <code>tsquery</code></a> machinery is a very good starting p=
oint. It is part of vanilla PostgreSQL, needs no extra moving parts, and wo=
rks extremely well for the vast majority of use-cases. Start there.</p><p>I=
f you outgrow it - typically because you need better relevance ranking (BM2=
5) or more scalability - you don=E2=80=99t have to leave PostgreSQL either.=
 There are extensions that pick up exactly where the built-in search ends:<=
/p><ul><li><a href=3D"https://github.com/timescale/pg_textsearch">pg_textse=
arch</a> by Timescale / TigerData - BM25 ranking as a PostgreSQL extension.=
</li><li><a href=3D"https://github.com/paradedb/paradedb">ParadeDB / pg_sea=
rch</a> - Elasticsearch-grade search inside PostgreSQL, built on Tantivy (<=
a href=3D"https://docs.paradedb.com/">docs</a>).</li></ul><p><u>That=E2=80=
=99s the beauty of it: You can start with plain vanilla PostgreSQL and seam=
lessly move to more advanced techniques and third-party extensions once - a=
nd only if - you actually need them.</u></p><p>The pros and cons of full-te=
xt search on PostgreSQL are discussed very nicely in <a href=3D"https://www=
.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7495362603527909377/">this Linked=
In discussion</a> - recommended reading before you decide.</p><p>More on th=
e topic: <a href=3D"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch.html=
">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch.html</a></p><h3 id=3D"=
postgresql-replaces-mongodb-excellent-json-support">PostgreSQL replaces Mon=
goDB: Excellent Json Support</h3><p>PostgreSQL has excellent support for <a=
 href=3D"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html">storin=
g and querying(!) json</a>. It also features an index type (GIN) that makes=
 these operations blazingly fast. <u>Is there a need for MongoDB any more?<=
/u>.</p><p>The Guardian also wrote an excellent article how they <a href=3D=
"https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/nov/30/bye-bye-mongo-hello-postgres"=
>switched from
Mongo to PostgreSQL</a>. Thanks for sharing <a href=3D"https://www.linkedin=
.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7163447276114317313?commentUrn=3Durn%3Ali%=
3Acomment%3A%28activity%3A7163447276114317313%2C7163615175755874304%29&amp;=
dashCommentUrn=3Durn%3Ali%3Afsd_comment%3A%287163615175755874304%2Curn%3Ali=
%3Aactivity%3A7163447276114317313%29">Jan-Otto</a>! Hazel also <a href=3D"h=
ttps://challahscript.com/what_i_wish_someone_told_me_about_postgres#jsonb-i=
s-a-sharp-knife">wrote a nice piece on jsonb</a> and what to take into acco=
unt when using it.</p><h3 id=3D"postgresql-replaces-kafka-and-rabbitmq-post=
gresql-as-a-queue">PostgreSQL replaces Kafka and RabbitMQ: PostgreSQL as a =
queue</h3><p>Events, queues and persistent logs are getting more and more i=
mportant in today=E2=80=99s software systems. Systems like Kafka, RabbitMQ,=
 SQS and others provide that functionality. But maintaining them is annoyin=
g, custom and you need the skillset.</p><p>The good news: You can just use =
PostgreSQL. The magic comes from</p><ul><li>SELECT .. FOR UPDATE</li><li>SE=
LECT .. SKIP LOCKED</li></ul><p>Using these SQL features you can effectivel=
y use a table as queue. Either in a persistent fashion with a cursor and ma=
ny consumers, or in a read-once fashion.</p><p>The article at <a href=3D"ht=
tps://www.crunchydata.com/blog/message-queuing-using-native-postgresql">cru=
nchydata explains this concept very well</a>.</p><p><u>My tip: Start with P=
ostgreSQL as a queueing system. Only when that does no longer perform well =
switch to other systems like Kafka, RabbitMQ or SQS. You=E2=80=99ll be surp=
rised how well PostgreSQL works.</u></p><h3 id=3D"postgresql-replaces-click=
house-high-volume-time-series-data">PostgreSQL Replaces Clickhouse: High Vo=
lume Time Series Data</h3><p>Time series data is special. Often you get man=
y data points in a very short amount of time. And then you have to aggregat=
e the data frequently, doing some statistics on it and so on.</p><p>There a=
re specialized software systems like Clickhouse (amazing by the way=E2=80=
=A6). But you can also use a plugin for PostgreSQL that allows you to do (n=
early) the same: <a href=3D"https://github.com/timescale/timescaledb">Times=
cale</a>.</p><p><u>I=E2=80=99ve used Timescale and can recommend it. The go=
od news is that you can continue using PostgreSQL - even for high volume da=
ta easily. No need to learn and maintain something new.</u></p><h3 id=3D"po=
stgresql-as-vector-database-for-ai-workflows">PostgreSQL as Vector Database=
 for AI Workflows</h3><p>Timescale lately released the <a href=3D"https://d=
ocs.timescale.com/use-timescale/latest/extensions/pgvector/?ref=3Dtimescale=
.com">pgvector extension</a>, that turns your PostgreSQL into a vector data=
base.
This allows you to use the tech you already know for indexing and retrieval=
 of relevant data. That=E2=80=99s an essential part of AI LLM workflows.</p=
><p>Timescale also recently announced <a href=3D"https://github.com/timesca=
le/pgai">pgai</a> that includes pgvector, but also a lot of other nice exte=
nsions that make it super simple to index data, call LLM models and retriev=
e data based on similarity.</p><h3 id=3D"postgresql-replaces-redis-non-pers=
istent-high-performance-caching">PostgreSQL Replaces Redis: Non-Persistent =
High Performance Caching</h3><p>Caching is important. Most applications use=
 something like Redis as a cache to get information like sessions and more =
quickly. A cache can by definition lose data and can be regenerated from th=
e original source.</p><p>But. Why use Redis when PostgreSQL can be tuned to=
 be as fast (in most usecases) as a Redis cache? The secret is using an UNL=
OGGED table. You can even emulate Redis=E2=80=99 automatic expire by a trig=
ger. <a href=3D"https://martinheinz.dev/blog/105">A lot has been written ab=
out this</a> - I can just recommend trying it out.</p><h3 id=3D"postgresql-=
replaces-file-system-for-raw-data">PostgreSQL Replaces File System: For Raw=
 Data</h3><p>For one of my clients we had to read and write a huge amount o=
f small pieces of binary encoded information. We initially thought that doi=
ng this via the file system was the fastest way to do so.</p><p>After some =
performance checks it became clear that PostgreSQL was even faster than rea=
ding from the file system for our use-case. PostgreSQL uses the file system=
 very efficiently for its data - and it adds a lot of caching and efficient=
 reading and writing strategies that can outperform writing and reading raw=
 data on a file system.</p><p>We used <a href=3D"https://github.com/google/=
flatbuffers">Flatbuffers</a> to store the data in a blob column. Data was t=
hen de-serialized on the client. You might want to try that approach as wel=
l.</p><h3 id=3D"postgresql-replacing-your-graph-database">PostgreSQL Replac=
ing Your Graph Database</h3><p>Hierarchical data can be managed in SQL via =
recursive queries. That=E2=80=99s ok, but also super-hard to read, maintain=
 and debug. Not even speaking of performance.</p><p>The better way is the <=
a href=3D"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ltree.html">LTREE datatyp=
e of PostgreSQL</a>. It helped me not only once to implement hierarchical t=
ag structures. Easy to read, maintain and blazingly fast.</p><h3 id=3D"post=
gresql-as-replacement-for-graphdb-and-neo4j">PostgreSQL as Replacement for =
GraphDB and Neo4J</h3><p>But what if you need a <em>real</em> graph - not j=
ust a tree? Nodes, edges, properties, and queries that traverse relationshi=
ps in every direction? That=E2=80=99s usually the moment someone suggests a=
dding Neo4j to the stack. And with it: another system to run, another backu=
p strategy, another data sync.</p><p>You don=E2=80=99t have to. <a href=3D"=
https://age.apache.org/">Apache AGE</a> (=E2=80=9CA Graph Extension=E2=80=
=9D) turns PostgreSQL into a graph database. It=E2=80=99s an Apache Softwar=
e Foundation top-level project and it implements <a href=3D"https://opencyp=
her.org/">openCypher</a> - the same query language you=E2=80=99d use in Neo=
4j. The best part: graph queries and plain SQL live in the <em>same</em> da=
tabase and can be combined in a single statement.</p><div class=3D"highligh=
t"><pre tabindex=3D"0" class=3D"chroma"><code class=3D"language-sql" data-l=
ang=3D"sql"><span class=3D"line"><span class=3D"cl"><span class=3D"k">SELEC=
T</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"=
w"> </span><span class=3D"k">FROM</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cla=
ss=3D"n">cypher</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s1">'my_grap=
h'</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"err">$$</span><span class=3D"w">
</span></span></span><span class=3D"line"><span class=3D"cl"><span class=3D=
"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">MATCH</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><spa=
n class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">a</span><span class=3D"p">:</span><=
span class=3D"n">Person</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"o">-=
</span><span class=3D"p">[:</span><span class=3D"n">WORKS_AT</span><span cl=
ass=3D"p">]</span><span class=3D"o">-&gt;</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><=
span class=3D"k">c</span><span class=3D"p">:</span><span class=3D"n">Compan=
y</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w">
</span></span></span><span class=3D"line"><span class=3D"cl"><span class=3D=
"w">    </span><span class=3D"k">RETURN</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><sp=
an class=3D"n">a</span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">name</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"k">c<=
/span><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"n">name</span><span class=3D=
"w">
</span></span></span><span class=3D"line"><span class=3D"cl"><span class=3D=
"err">$$</span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span cl=
ass=3D"k">AS</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"p">(</span><spa=
n class=3D"n">person</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">agty=
pe</span><span class=3D"p">,</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D=
"n">company</span><span class=3D"w"> </span><span class=3D"n">agtype</span>=
<span class=3D"p">);</span><span class=3D"w">
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p><u>My tip - the same one as for =
queueing: Start with PostgreSQL. Model your graph with AGE (or with LTREE i=
f a hierarchy is all you need) and only reach for a dedicated graph databas=
e if you really hit the limits. Your data stays in one place, transactional=
 and consistent with the rest of your application.</u></p><p>More on the to=
pic: <a href=3D"https://age.apache.org/age-manual/master/index.html">Apache=
 AGE documentation</a> and the <a href=3D"https://github.com/apache/age">so=
urce on GitHub</a>.</p><h3 id=3D"postgresql-replacing-your-microservice">Po=
stgreSQL Replacing Your Microservice</h3><p>Most of the =E2=80=9Cmicroservi=
ces=E2=80=9D these days are only about models, getting data from a database=
 and returning json to the client.</p><p>But you know what? PostgreSQL can =
turn any query into a Json result. That effectively replaces your server mi=
ddleware. There are Pros and Cons to this approach, but it shows the capabi=
lities of PostgreSQL. The amazing <a href=3D"https://blog.jooq.org/stop-map=
ping-stuff-in-your-middleware-use-sqls-xml-or-json-operators-instead/">Luka=
s Eder wrote about the topic</a> - not PostgreSQL specific - but everything=
 mentioned there is very well doable in PostgreSQL as well</p><h3 id=3D"pos=
tgresql---replacing-your-playstation-5">PostgreSQL - Replacing your Playsta=
tion 5</h3><p>Well. Some enthusiast implemented <a href=3D"https://github.c=
om/nuno-faria/tetris-sql/blob/main/game.sql">Tetris as Common Table Express=
ions in pure SQL</a>. Crazy. And maybe not to be taken too seriously.</p><h=
2 id=3D"conclusion">Conclusion</h2><p>The list above is not very exhaustive=
. PostgreSQL is a very flexible piece of software. And it can be extended w=
ith plugins to do more and more.</p><p>You need simplicity if you want to m=
ove fast. If you come across a new requirement always ask: Can=E2=80=99t Po=
stgreSQL do this? And do we really need that shiny new technology X?</p><p>=
PostgreSQL might not be the answer to everything - but it is the answer to =
a lot more than you might think!</p></div><h2>Related posts</h2><ul><li><a =
href=3D"https://www.raphaelbauer.com/posts/three-strikes-one-on-one/">Three=
